# Rules for any AI assistant working in this folder

**These rules are for someone RUNNING the QA kit.** The maintainer works in the source
repository, which is the copy that contains a `.aitp-maintainer` file at its root; if
that file is present, this page does not apply and the normal development workflow
does.

If you are an AI assistant and `.aitp-maintainer` is not there, everything below
applies to you.

---

## This kit is read-only

The person using it runs it. They do not change it. One maintainer changes it, in a
different copy, deliberately.

**You may:**

- read anything in `docs/` and explain it;
- help write or fix the user's own two files — `config/apps/<their-slug>/*.json` and
  `config/env/<their-env>.json`;
- explain an error, in plain words, using `docs/`;
- help them compose the message they send to the maintainer.

**You may not:**

- edit, move, delete, rename, reformat or regenerate **any** other tracked file —
  this includes `packages/`, `apps/`, `scripts/`, `tests/`, `playwright.config.ts`,
  `package.json`, `pnpm-lock.yaml`, every `tsconfig*.json`, `infra/`, `.gitignore`,
  `.gitattributes`, `.cursorignore`, `.cursor/`, this file, `kit-manifest.json`,
  `config/apps/dms/`, `config/apps/bundled-demo/` and the four existing
  `config/env/*.json` files;
- run any command whose effect is to change those files. Including, and not limited
  to: `sed -i`, `git checkout`, `git restore`, `git reset`, `git clean`, `git stash`,
  `pnpm add`, `pnpm remove`, `pnpm update`, `pnpm install <package>`,
  `prettier --write`, `eslint --fix`, `npm`/`yarn` equivalents, or a script you wrote
  that writes to one of them;
- read, open, print, echo, summarise or quote `.env`. Not once, not partially, not
  "to check the format" — `.env.example` is the template and it has no values in it;
- set `ALLOW_WRITES`, in a command, a file, a shell profile or anywhere else;
- write to the QA's workbook. It is read-only input. Never open it for writing, never
  "update the Status column";
- paste the workbook, a capture, a report, a screenshot of the application, or
  anything out of `artifacts/` into a chat or an external service.

## When something fails

**Explain it from `docs/`, and then stop.** Do not fix code. The right next action is
almost always: tell the user to send the exact error text to **Hashim**.

`docs/QA-QUICKSTART.md` and `docs/ADD-AN-APPLICATION.md` describe every command and
what each refusal means. `docs/WRITING-STEPS.md` covers how to write a test step the
framework can run. If the answer is not in those, say so — a guess that sends somebody
editing framework code is worse than "I don't know".

## If you see "Cannot find module", "MODULE_NOT_FOUND", or a missing kit file

**The kit is damaged. It is not a dependency problem and it is not a bug to fix.**

Any of these, especially soon after extracting the zip, means a file or a folder the
kit needs is not where it belongs:

```
Error: Cannot find module '@aitp/execution-engine'
code: 'MODULE_NOT_FOUND'
```

```
Cannot find module '.../scripts/kit-check.mjs'
```

**Do not:** run `pnpm install`, rename a folder, restore or re-create a file, edit
`package.json` or any `tsconfig*.json`, or try to make the import resolve. Every one
of those is a repair to a kit nobody can vouch for afterwards, and the results it
then produces look exactly like results from an intact one.

**Do:** tell the user to re-extract the original zip into a clean folder and copy
back only `config/apps/<slug>/`, `config/env/<file>.json` and `.env` — then send the
**full error text** to Hashim.

One case the kit cannot report on itself, stated plainly rather than papered over:
if `scripts/kit-check.mjs` is the file that is missing, the error comes from Node
rather than from the kit, so there is no `KIT MODIFIED` message. The command still
does not run. Treat it exactly as above.

## If you see "KIT MODIFIED"

That message means a locked file no longer matches `kit-manifest.json`. It is the
kit's integrity check refusing, on purpose.

**Never try to make it go away.** Do not edit `kit-manifest.json`, do not regenerate
it, do not delete it, do not create `.aitp-maintainer`, do not pass a flag, do not
set an environment variable, and do not suggest any of those. There is no supported
way to skip it and inventing one would make every result the kit produces worthless.

The recovery is in the message and it is the only one: re-extract the original zip,
then copy back only `config/apps/<slug>/`, `config/env/<file>.json` and `.env`. Or
contact Hashim.

## Why it is written this way

A QA reading a stack trace will reasonably ask an assistant to fix it, and an
assistant that edits framework code produces a kit whose results nobody can trust —
including the QA, who has no way to know. Refusing to help with code is not
unhelpfulness here; it is the only way the test results stay worth anything.
